1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Trusted tap water kills more than a hundred.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
A century-old college tradition turns tragic.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
American tanks turn out to be death traps.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Fourteen die when a building under construction self-destructs.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
And the Great Airport Luggage Massacre.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Tap water.

8
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,940
As it flows from spigots in our kitchens and bathrooms,

9
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:53,720
we expect it to be safe to drink.

10
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:57,340
We trust treatment plants to remove any harmful impurities

11
00:00:57,341 --> 00:00:59,180
that might work their way into the system.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,320
But in March 1993, hundreds of thousands of Milwaukee residents became ill from drinking

13
00:01:12,321 --> 00:01:15,680
their household water.

14
00:01:15,681 --> 00:01:23,840
Grocers sold out of bottled H2O, and Milwaukeeans anxiously lined up to receive purified water.

15
00:01:23,841 --> 00:01:29,760
One local health official in early April described the situation in Milwaukee as a near panic

16
00:01:29,761 --> 00:01:31,900
because so many people were going to their doctors,

17
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:35,900
so many people were showing up at emergency rooms and hospitals

18
00:01:35,901 --> 00:01:37,980
with intestinal illnesses.

19
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:39,440
We had all been asked,

20
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:42,240
something going on, my neighbor's sick, my child's sick,

21
00:01:42,241 --> 00:01:44,300
and actually as we began to look around,

22
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:46,380
we noticed a number of coworkers weren't around,

23
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:50,260
and that's kind of a flag to us that in fact it's more than just a flu,

24
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:52,660
and maybe something actually happening.

25
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:55,820
Within two months, 100 died,

26
00:01:55,821 --> 00:02:01,440
and more than 400,000 suffered illnesses ranging from diarrhea to fever to vomiting.

27
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:06,780
I think it's important for people to take that precaution and boil water,

28
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:10,200
either for drinking or for washing food.

29
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:14,100
Investigators discovered the majority of suffering residents

30
00:02:14,101 --> 00:02:16,880
were receiving water from the Howard Avenue Treatment Plant,

31
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:22,360
one of two facilities serving 800,000 Milwaukee citizens.

32
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:27,020
The Howard plant treated more than 100 million gallons of water per day.

33
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:33,340
Its main supply intake was located 3 1⁄2 miles away in Lake Michigan.

34
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:37,580
Was there indeed something in the water causing the problem?

35
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:46,160
The first week of April, a physician tested a specimen from a patient

36
00:02:46,161 --> 00:02:50,440
for a specific parasite, the protozoan cryptosporidium,

37
00:02:50,441 --> 00:02:54,740
and found it, contacted other physicians and health officials.

38
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:59,940
They tested other samples, confirmed that it was cryptosporidium.

39
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:05,020
Cryptosporidium, shown here, is a microscopic parasite,

40
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:08,220
more commonly found in animals than human beings.

41
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:10,740
It's a one-celled creature.

42
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:14,940
It multiplies in the intestines of infected humans or animals.

43
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,980
It comes out of those intestines in the feces,

44
00:03:17,981 --> 00:03:22,200
comes out in a little egg-like packet called an oocyst,

45
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:25,520
and that's the infectious stage for the next person who ingests it.

46
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,620
Tests of water samples from the Howard Avenue plant

47
00:03:29,621 --> 00:03:31,580
showed the presence of cryptosporidium.

48
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:38,920
The parasites may have entered the water as the result of several factors.

49
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:42,760
And those things that I think about as occurring

50
00:03:42,761 --> 00:03:48,580
were heavy rains falling on icy ground,

51
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:51,280
so there's a lot of water runoff into rivers,

52
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:53,820
creating a lot of muddy water,

53
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:57,980
also potentially bringing in cryptospiridium motocyst,

54
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:01,200
the infectious stage of cryptospiridium, into those waters.

55
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:06,220
Then that water being taken into a water treatment plant

56
00:04:06,221 --> 00:04:10,080
that wasn't filtering their water in an optimal way.

57
00:04:10,081 --> 00:04:16,080
Filters at Milwaukee's Howard Avenue treatment plant, like those in most of the U.S.,

58
00:04:16,081 --> 00:04:21,380
were made up of layers of fine-grained sand designed to catch any foreign substance.

59
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:26,380
Milwaukee health officials acknowledged that the sand had not been maintained properly.

60
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,720
Filter beds needed to be cleaned. They could have been cleaned more often.

61
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:36,120
There are turbidity monitors and sensors that could have been placed in more spots along the process,

62
00:04:36,121 --> 00:04:39,800
so you would have had more continuous monitoring of the water quality.

63
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:43,540
We had more of an intermittent monitoring of the water quality.

64
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,080
You need to think of a water treatment plant filter more like a bucket

65
00:04:48,081 --> 00:04:50,940
with holes punched in the bottom full of sand.

66
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:55,560
This bucket happens to be a 20-foot by 20-foot pool, but it's the same principle.

67
00:04:56,440 --> 00:04:59,720
And no matter how tightly you pack those little grains of sand,

68
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:04,100
there's still microscopic holes in those grains of sand.

69
00:05:04,101 --> 00:05:09,060
And those holes are much bigger than the O-O cyst.

70
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:13,380
In order for sand filters to work properly,

71
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:16,260
water must first be treated with chemicals

72
00:05:16,261 --> 00:05:19,280
to make the tiniest impurities bond with others,

73
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,180
to form particles large enough to be caught by the filtration system.

74
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:28,720
This bonding process was not working sufficiently in Milwaukee,

75
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:33,000
allowing cryptosporidium to slip through the cracks.

76
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:39,100
There were upgrades to the plant that could have been made that hadn't.

77
00:05:39,101 --> 00:05:41,100
Milwaukee was very proud of its water.

78
00:05:41,101 --> 00:05:43,100
We'd been making products from water.

79
00:05:43,101 --> 00:05:47,100
We'd been drinking clean water out of Lake Michigan for well over 100 years.

80
00:05:47,101 --> 00:05:53,100
And in retrospect, I think we were a little complacent that those improvements hadn't been made.

81
00:05:53,101 --> 00:05:55,100
There was no reason to make them.

82
00:05:55,101 --> 00:05:59,100
There was nothing to believe that anything was wrong.

83
00:05:59,101 --> 00:06:03,100
Once Cryptosporidium was determined to be the cause of the problem,

84
00:06:03,101 --> 00:06:06,760
Then came the tougher question, how to get rid of it.

85
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:14,860
Just as the process for cryptosporidium to enter the system was highly complex, so was the fix.

86
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:19,060
They made a lot of changes in their whole operation.

87
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:26,620
Number one, they did correct the coagulation pretreatment process, so they got the turbidity down where it should have been.

88
00:06:26,621 --> 00:06:35,620
Number two, they invested a lot of dollars in a new chemical treatment process that would kill oocysts called ozonization.

89
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:43,160
Ozone is a much more powerful disinfectant than chlorine is, but it's very expensive to produce.

90
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:48,040
And a third major change they made is changing the location of their water intake.

91
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:56,040
Prior to the outbreak, the water intake was close to where the rivers and sewage plant discharged their water.

92
00:06:56,620 --> 00:06:59,920
and they were drawing some of that water back in that intake.

93
00:06:59,921 --> 00:07:04,120
They now move that intake quite a ways away from that.

94
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:09,000
The Center for Great Lakes Studies and the Great Lakes Research Facility,

95
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:10,980
which is behind us here on the Inner Harbor,

96
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:17,320
they participated in a study that indicated that if they extended that water intake pipe

97
00:07:17,321 --> 00:07:22,580
less than a mile, just eight-tenths of a mile more, out into the lake,

98
00:07:22,581 --> 00:07:27,460
they would avoid 98% or more of the possible pollutants

99
00:07:27,461 --> 00:07:29,680
that would be flowing along the near shoreline.

100
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:32,920
So that moved them out of that plume of contaminants

101
00:07:32,921 --> 00:07:35,200
that regularly flow along the shoreline.

102
00:07:35,201 --> 00:07:40,360
So that also helped avoid another outbreak in some future spring.

103
00:07:42,760 --> 00:07:43,840
That was super.

104
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,240
When the fix was complete in Milwaukee,

105
00:07:48,940 --> 00:07:50,600
the Howard Water Treatment Plant

106
00:07:50,601 --> 00:07:52,560
became one of the most advanced in the nation.

107
00:07:53,700 --> 00:07:58,440
But the solution came at great cost, both in lives and dollars.

108
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:02,000
There's been a lot of debate whether or not the people of Milwaukee

109
00:08:02,001 --> 00:08:05,640
needed to spend $80 million to convert their water treatment plants

110
00:08:05,641 --> 00:08:07,240
to the ozone process.

111
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:09,920
But given the intensity of this outbreak,

112
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:13,100
the number of people that were affected the deaths that we mentioned,

113
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:15,400
the people of this town really demanded

114
00:08:15,401 --> 00:08:18,840
that the water treatment plants in Milwaukee be upgraded

115
00:08:18,841 --> 00:08:23,180
to the best of their ability, and we're willing to spend the $80 million to do so.

116
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:28,980
The delicate balance between risk and expense will always be a source of controversy.

117
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:33,620
But Milwaukee public health officials are confident they've made the right decision.

118
00:08:34,420 --> 00:08:35,840
They know the alternative.

119
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:38,480
People ask me this all the time.

120
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:41,280
Will it ever happen again? Could it ever happen again?

121
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:45,260
Well, the ozonation process that we've installed makes it very unlikely.

122
00:08:45,261 --> 00:08:50,120
but I still have enough public health and science in me to say you never say never.

123
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,120
The Milwaukee water treatment disaster made many American cities examine and upgrade their own methods of filtration.

124
00:08:58,860 --> 00:09:03,340
Few have gone to the trouble and expense of a total system overhaul like that of Milwaukee,

125
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:09,020
but there is no doubt that the quality of tap water across the nation has improved

126
00:09:09,021 --> 00:09:13,200
as the result of one city's tragic engineering disaster.

127
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:22,440
Boiling tap water for at least one minute kills cryptosporidium parasites.

128
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,300
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

129
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:34,660
Texas A&M University in College Station, Texas, is a school of honorable tradition.

130
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:44,000
Once an all-male military school, the now co-ed campus still reflects a history based on discipline, motivation, and pride.

131
00:09:45,260 --> 00:09:59,248
One of the most popular and long-standing traditions has been the elaborate building and torching of a giant bonfire the night before Texas A&M plays football against

132
00:09:59,249 --> 00:10:01,860
arch-rival University of Texas.

133
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:14,000
A tradition which turned tragic in 1999 with a collapse during bonfire construction, resulting in the deaths of 12 students.

134
00:10:15,260 --> 00:10:19,900
The disaster brought an end to the annual Aggie Bonfire,

135
00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:25,300
the first of which burned in 1909 as a pile of rubble.

136
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:30,300
You can look at the pictures from year to year of the bonfire

137
00:10:30,301 --> 00:10:35,520
and see where it grew not only in size and shape,

138
00:10:35,740 --> 00:10:39,600
but it was also made of different materials, starting with the trash.

139
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:42,900
Then they switched to lumber, which was originally cut off the university

140
00:10:42,901 --> 00:10:44,500
and then cut off of private land.

141
00:10:44,501 --> 00:10:57,436
And actually one of the evolutions that occurred in the whole bonfire activity was that the building of the stack became a minor part of the entire Aggie Bonfire

142
00:10:57,437 --> 00:10:58,320
experience.

143
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:04,660
The actual cutting of the wood was a more involved activity.

144
00:11:09,269 --> 00:11:19,848
By the time Jim McTasney became head Red Pot, or bonfire organizer, in 1992, students hand-cut logs at nearby forests needing to be cleared, trucked them to the campus

145
00:11:19,849 --> 00:11:29,540
bonfire site, then spent weeks lashing the logs together with steel wire, slowly filling out a stack built around a center pole supported by guide ropes.

146
00:11:29,541 --> 00:11:35,040
as many as 3,000 students worked on building the stack.

147
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:39,420
The purpose of Aggie Bonfire to the students that built it

148
00:11:39,421 --> 00:11:41,860
was not to build a stack.

149
00:11:42,180 --> 00:11:44,860
Provide answers to why this tragedy occurred.

150
00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:49,160
It was to go out there and experience leadership,

151
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:54,400
and in order for Aggie Bonfire to be the valuable leadership experience that it was,

152
00:11:54,401 --> 00:11:56,680
and the valuable training ground that it was,

153
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:59,140
you had to keep the physical labor in it.

154
00:11:59,540 --> 00:12:03,140
You purposefully shunned the mechanization and the automation

155
00:12:03,141 --> 00:12:06,920
in order to allow as many students as possible to participate.

156
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:13,140
According to tradition, if the bonfire burned past midnight,

157
00:12:14,600 --> 00:12:17,680
the Aggies would win the game against the University of Texas.

158
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:24,500
So emphasis in building the stack was placed not so much on height

159
00:12:24,501 --> 00:12:29,420
as its aesthetic appeal and ability to burn into the wee hours of the morning.

160
00:12:29,540 --> 00:12:40,460
Larry Gross, a professor at Texas A&M from 1981 through 1996, used his construction management

161
00:12:40,461 --> 00:12:44,700
background to help students design a structure that could look good and burn long.

162
00:12:46,460 --> 00:12:52,820
I did go out and observe the bonfire and the lighting of it and also the collapse of it

163
00:12:52,821 --> 00:12:53,860
after it was ignited.

164
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:58,700
And we noticed how the rotation, it did rotate as it came down.

165
00:12:59,540 --> 00:13:12,160
To eliminate that rotation action, we suggested to interlock by having the logs from the top layer extend into the lower layer, space them throughout.

166
00:13:12,161 --> 00:13:18,520
And this would help to lock the two stacks together, and this would eliminate that rotation action.

167
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:24,700
Larry and Red Pot student organizers analyzed other structural issues as well.

168
00:13:24,701 --> 00:13:30,980
We suggested that they move out from the center pole at 20 or 25 feet

169
00:13:30,981 --> 00:13:35,140
and drill a series of holes all the way around the bonfire

170
00:13:35,141 --> 00:13:37,760
and set the poles into the ground,

171
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:41,580
and this would help to stabilize that bottom layer of them.

172
00:13:42,020 --> 00:13:46,060
And then we would fill it and take a cable and cinch it up.

173
00:13:46,061 --> 00:13:51,420
And by doing that, it really made a good structural and strong bonfire then.

174
00:13:51,421 --> 00:13:56,620
Larry Gross viewed building the bonfire as a valuable educational process,

175
00:13:56,621 --> 00:14:01,460
so much so that bonfire design became an independent study class.

176
00:14:01,461 --> 00:14:08,560
There were a number of young students who had an issue of, obviously, a budget issue.

177
00:14:08,561 --> 00:14:11,320
They had to follow. They had a schedule.

178
00:14:11,321 --> 00:14:14,820
It had to be ready to burn the night before the football game.

179
00:14:14,821 --> 00:14:16,160
They had to be safe.

180
00:14:16,161 --> 00:14:19,620
So it had all the makings of a major construction project.

181
00:14:19,621 --> 00:14:25,640
And the class project was to develop a procedures manual for constructing a bonfire.

182
00:14:26,940 --> 00:14:32,560
Photos of bonfires during this time period suggest the obvious attention to structural detail.

183
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:36,920
But then came November 18, 1999.

184
00:14:38,380 --> 00:14:42,620
Students worked throughout the night to build the bonfire, scheduled to burn a week later.

185
00:14:42,621 --> 00:14:49,680
At 2 a.m., as cranes lifted logs to those working on the top and the sides of the stack,

186
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:52,120
the unimaginable happened.

187
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:55,580
The entire structure collapsed.

188
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:03,120
The result was tragic.

189
00:15:04,280 --> 00:15:08,460
Despite the efforts of rescue workers to remove the heavy logs as quickly as possible,

190
00:15:09,320 --> 00:15:11,020
12 students were found dead.

191
00:15:11,021 --> 00:15:13,380
27 were injured

192
00:15:13,381 --> 00:15:22,340
following 90 years of successful aggie bonfire building

193
00:15:22,341 --> 00:15:24,500
what went wrong

194
00:15:24,501 --> 00:15:28,660
texas a&m established a commission to determine the answer

195
00:15:28,661 --> 00:15:32,800
according to the commission report

196
00:15:32,801 --> 00:15:35,460
the collapse occurred for several reasons

197
00:15:35,461 --> 00:15:39,240
as comparisons with earlier bonfire stacks reveal

198
00:15:39,241 --> 00:15:51,181
A decision had been made in 1999 to try to make the tiers more straight up, vertical, as opposed to earlier teepee-like shapes, in which the logs leaned slightly

199
00:15:51,182 --> 00:15:51,882
inward.

200
00:15:53,400 --> 00:16:04,020
The logs used in 1999 appeared to be more irregularly shaped than those used in previous years, providing a looser stack, harder to contain.

201
00:16:04,021 --> 00:16:11,100
aggressive wedging of logs on the second tier to fill gaps below

202
00:16:11,101 --> 00:16:16,140
increased pressure on steel wire encircling the stacks

203
00:16:16,141 --> 00:16:19,180
causing it to snap under pressure

204
00:16:19,181 --> 00:16:27,380
using the analogy of a wooden barrel the hoop strength was not sufficient

205
00:16:27,381 --> 00:16:39,780
some of the issues that we had developed with our students and with other faculty not just myself

206
00:16:39,781 --> 00:16:43,500
but other faculty in our department were involved with this

207
00:16:43,501 --> 00:16:48,160
apparently they some of these activities were not being followed

208
00:16:48,161 --> 00:16:55,960
the commission's report concluded that the tragedy resulted from a lack of engineering supervision

209
00:16:55,961 --> 00:17:01,920
surprising to many in view of how the structure increased in size and complexity over the years.

210
00:17:03,460 --> 00:17:08,300
The Aggie Bonfire had always been a project run by students for the students.

211
00:17:09,900 --> 00:17:14,440
Larry Gross's involvement had been considered a casual, informal student-teacher collaboration.

212
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:21,860
The collapse was about physical failures driven by organizational failures

213
00:17:21,861 --> 00:17:27,340
whose origins span decades of administrations, faculty, and students.

214
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:31,900
No single factor can explain the collapse,

215
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:38,460
just as no single change will ensure that a tragedy like this never happens again.

216
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:44,280
1999 was the last year the bonfire was built.

217
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:48,820
Today, a memorial honoring the students killed in the collapse is being built,

218
00:17:48,821 --> 00:17:50,700
where the bonfire once burned.

219
00:17:51,860 --> 00:17:56,920
It is unlikely there will ever be another Aggie Bonfire.

220
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:01,960
For student red pots like Jim McTasney, that's a major loss.

221
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:10,940
Any success that I have today, I can trace back to my experience with Aggie Bonfire.

222
00:18:11,580 --> 00:18:20,780
It was the most rewarding and educational leadership experience that I had at this university and have had since.

223
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:31,580
Aggie bonfires in the 1990s contained between 5,000 to 7,000 logs

224
00:18:31,581 --> 00:18:34,480
that were 8 to 24 inches in diameter.

225
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:39,200
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

226
00:18:42,420 --> 00:18:46,880
How many times have you seen images like this from film shot during World War II?

227
00:18:46,881 --> 00:18:55,600
powerful american sherman tanks rolling across the european countryside

228
00:18:57,680 --> 00:19:01,120
delivering firepower to allied ground troops where it was needed most

229
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:09,040
the sherman tanks weight class was somewhere between a light tank which was small and a heavy

230
00:19:09,041 --> 00:19:15,280
tank was a big monster it was designed to go over military bridges it was designed to fit on railroad

231
00:19:15,281 --> 00:19:18,081
cars. It was designed for easy transportation.

232
00:19:18,082 --> 00:19:20,700
It was designed for automotive reliability.

233
00:19:21,300 --> 00:19:25,939
When the decision was made to invade Europe, General George S.

234
00:19:25,940 --> 00:19:27,660
Patton himself selected

235
00:19:27,661 --> 00:19:32,120
the Sherman as his tank of choice. He said, we don't need a heavy tank.

236
00:19:32,121 --> 00:19:33,000
We can win the

237
00:19:33,001 --> 00:19:37,014
war with this medium tank. Make as many of those as you can.

238
00:19:37,015 --> 00:19:38,620
Sherman tanks rolled off

239
00:19:38,621 --> 00:19:43,053
American assembly lines by the thousands, nearly 50,000 total.

240
00:19:43,054 --> 00:19:44,840
When they reached Europe,

241
00:19:45,640 --> 00:19:48,400
three to five-man tank crews immediately took them into battle.

242
00:19:49,860 --> 00:19:54,640
The image of the approaching Shermans became a myth-like representation of the invincibility

243
00:19:54,641 --> 00:19:56,823
of America's war machine.

244
00:19:56,824 --> 00:20:02,500
But those who actually fought in and maintained the Sherman tanks

245
00:20:02,501 --> 00:20:06,100
and claimed the invincibility was a myth.

246
00:20:07,140 --> 00:20:09,940
And as far as their design for World War II was concerned,

247
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:12,940
the tanks were engineering disasters.

248
00:20:17,140 --> 00:20:19,480
General Patton was quite a historian.

249
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:22,000
He knew a lot about wars and the history of wars.

250
00:20:24,920 --> 00:20:30,240
He honestly believed that white tanks that were fast and mobile

251
00:20:30,241 --> 00:20:32,240
and could cut in behind the enemy.

252
00:20:32,360 --> 00:20:34,240
The tanks were not supposed to fight tanks.

253
00:20:34,940 --> 00:20:37,120
Now that is a very unrealistic view.

254
00:20:37,260 --> 00:20:38,980
You cannot put a tank in the field

255
00:20:38,981 --> 00:20:41,340
without preparing it to defend itself

256
00:20:41,341 --> 00:20:42,920
and to be able to fight other tanks.

257
00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:45,420
So he was basically off base there.

258
00:20:46,780 --> 00:20:48,660
Belton Cooper served as a captain

259
00:20:48,661 --> 00:20:50,220
on a Sherman tank maintenance unit

260
00:20:50,221 --> 00:20:51,980
in the American 3rd Armored Division.

261
00:20:53,260 --> 00:20:55,020
He recounted his experiences

262
00:20:55,021 --> 00:20:57,580
in a book titled Death Traps.

263
00:20:57,581 --> 00:21:02,820
Cooper vividly remembers the first time he approached a disabled Sherman tank.

264
00:21:04,160 --> 00:21:05,420
I was devastated.

265
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:09,100
The first shot had gone through the transmission,

266
00:21:09,640 --> 00:21:12,200
and in addition to the four inches of armor in the transmission,

267
00:21:12,340 --> 00:21:18,320
it had gone through a five-inch steel driveshaft, and that stopped the tank.

268
00:21:18,440 --> 00:21:23,760
The second shot hit the top of the turret and cut a three-inch groove through about three inches of armor,

269
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:25,920
and the blast killed the tank gunner.

270
00:21:27,580 --> 00:21:32,760
When I saw that, I thought this tank had just had a protection hole.

271
00:21:32,880 --> 00:21:37,820
And after that, they started bringing tanks in droves where they're dead and wounded inside.

272
00:21:37,940 --> 00:21:39,140
And it was just terrible.

273
00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:43,100
And I knew then that we had an inferior tank.

274
00:21:47,100 --> 00:21:54,600
We had short-barreled guns designed to shoot high-explosive shells at a fairly low speed, 2,000 feet a second.

275
00:21:54,601 --> 00:22:02,160
The German guns would shoot a solid shot at 3,500 feet per second.

276
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:08,980
That meant that they had two, three times the penetrating power of our armor-piercing shells.

277
00:22:09,440 --> 00:22:12,960
And so they went through our tanks like a hot night through the butter.

278
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:18,520
The Shermans may have had the speed and mobility so highly regarded by Patton,

279
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:24,280
but those were not the qualities needed to fight the larger German Tiger and Panther tanks.

280
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:29,720
The statistics from Belton Cooper's unit speak for themselves.

281
00:22:30,440 --> 00:22:34,680
We lost 648 tanks totally destroyed in combat.

282
00:22:35,560 --> 00:22:39,380
Another 700 knocked out and repaired and put back in action.

283
00:22:39,900 --> 00:22:43,880
That's 1,348 tanks knocked out in combat.

284
00:22:44,320 --> 00:22:46,080
So that's 580% loss.

285
00:22:46,280 --> 00:22:48,560
I don't think anybody took that kind of loss in the war.

286
00:22:48,760 --> 00:22:52,060
Army, Navy, Air Force, submarines, nothing like that.

287
00:22:52,061 --> 00:22:57,060
In one unit, they sent 18 of these newly crewed tanks

288
00:22:57,160 --> 00:22:59,140
into combat, and in the first 20 minutes,

289
00:22:59,240 --> 00:23:00,540
17 of them were lost.

290
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:01,440
It was murder.

291
00:23:09,740 --> 00:23:13,640
It really was almost criminal what our forces had to put up

292
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:15,780
with because we didn't have the right tanks.

293
00:23:19,080 --> 00:23:21,260
Why were the Shermans so vulnerable?

294
00:23:21,261 --> 00:23:25,100
For starters, their offensive power was hindered by inadequate weaponry.

295
00:23:25,101 --> 00:23:32,040
I have a letter in my file that came from General Eisenhower, and he wrote directly to General

296
00:23:32,041 --> 00:23:35,280
Rose, and he said, General Rose, I know you've been losing a lot of tanks.

297
00:23:35,281 --> 00:23:35,981
I'd like to have

298
00:23:35,982 --> 00:23:41,600
you ask your crew and your tank commanders and your combat commanders, why are we losing

299
00:23:41,601 --> 00:23:43,068
so many Sherman tanks?

300
00:23:43,069 --> 00:23:47,540
And the best answer he gave was by a tank gunner who said, General,

301
00:23:47,541 --> 00:23:50,400
in order to knock out a German tank,

302
00:23:51,080 --> 00:23:54,180
I've got to get within 600 yards and catch it on the flank.

303
00:23:55,060 --> 00:23:57,560
They can knock me out at 2,000 yards head on.

304
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:00,720
For all practical purposes,

305
00:24:01,720 --> 00:24:04,100
a Sherman could successfully battle a German tank

306
00:24:04,101 --> 00:24:07,760
from just one direction.

307
00:24:08,220 --> 00:24:10,920
The only way our tanks could defeat their tanks

308
00:24:10,921 --> 00:24:12,800
is if we could run around behind them

309
00:24:12,801 --> 00:24:15,580
and shoot them in the rear end where there wasn't much armor.

310
00:24:15,581 --> 00:24:27,020
We did that, but typically it would take five tanks being blown up before the sixth one got around to shoot a German tank at the back.

311
00:24:27,860 --> 00:24:29,500
The mathematics were not good.

312
00:24:30,700 --> 00:24:36,140
When it became clear to American tank commanders that their magnificent war machines were so vulnerable,

313
00:24:37,500 --> 00:24:44,060
they resorted to makeshift methods that would add defensive capabilities and length to their lives.

314
00:24:45,580 --> 00:24:50,260
anything that they could gather that they could put on that faceplate to protect it

315
00:24:50,261 --> 00:24:56,380
they even put logs they put steel plates they put chicken wire backed up with four inches of

316
00:24:56,381 --> 00:25:00,580
concrete on the faceplate anything they could get on that to give them a threat of survival

317
00:25:00,581 --> 00:25:09,460
but for the most part any effort to improve the shermans was futile to make matters worse

318
00:25:09,461 --> 00:25:17,580
sherman's required highly flammable high octane gasoline their engines were originally designed

319
00:25:17,581 --> 00:25:23,740
for airplanes in every decision that was made in the development and use of the sherman from

320
00:25:23,741 --> 00:25:30,500
beginning to end they had to make choices between options okay we need a high-powered engine with

321
00:25:30,501 --> 00:25:35,440
lightweight okay we use this engine it's an aviation engine it burns more flammable fuel

322
00:25:35,441 --> 00:25:38,680
but power is the most important thing, power and weight.

323
00:25:38,840 --> 00:25:42,540
Or we need a tank that can move very fast to exploit a breakthrough

324
00:25:42,541 --> 00:25:45,420
so we can't have real heavy armor because that would slow it down.

325
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:47,860
We'll have just enough armor for what we think we need.

326
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:52,760
The job of a Sherman tank maintenance engineer was a grisly one.

327
00:25:53,520 --> 00:25:55,180
We had to clean the tanks out.

328
00:25:55,240 --> 00:25:59,100
It's a terrible job to clean out a tank with the head shot off

329
00:25:59,101 --> 00:26:01,320
and the arms and legs dismembered.

330
00:26:01,400 --> 00:26:02,180
It's just awful.

331
00:26:02,181 --> 00:26:08,860
And we had to put them in inside the tanks to get the bodies out and wrap them up in a tarp and then we put them all aside.

332
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:15,160
And the tank crews knew that when they got in that tank and went to battle, if they got hit, they were going to be in bad shape.

333
00:26:15,980 --> 00:26:24,280
For soldiers like Belton Cooper, whose World War II duties involved tank maintenance, there is no doubt who the unsung heroes of World War II are.

334
00:26:25,820 --> 00:26:27,880
The crews of Sherman tanks.

335
00:26:27,881 --> 00:26:35,420
their tanks were knocked out and sent back to repair and then they came back and they got into

336
00:26:35,421 --> 00:26:41,960
a tank that had just been freshly painted to cover over the bloodstains that had obvious patches in

337
00:26:41,961 --> 00:26:48,140
it from where the mechanics had plugged the holes they knew what they were going up against and then

338
00:26:48,141 --> 00:26:52,860
they went out and did it again it was the most heroic thing i can imagine because they knew they

339
00:26:52,861 --> 00:26:56,600
They knew that if they ran up against a Tiger or a Panther tank,

340
00:26:56,700 --> 00:26:58,360
they were in a lot of trouble.

341
00:26:58,460 --> 00:27:00,400
And they still got in those tanks every day,

342
00:27:00,500 --> 00:27:03,140
and they went out and beat Germany.

343
00:27:03,240 --> 00:27:06,140
Sherman tanks, remembered by their designers

344
00:27:06,240 --> 00:27:09,580
for speed and agility, and by their crews

345
00:27:09,680 --> 00:27:14,120
for being the right tanks for the wrong war.

346
00:27:16,320 --> 00:27:18,180
We were just inadequate.

347
00:27:18,280 --> 00:27:19,780
That's all I can say.

348
00:27:19,781 --> 00:27:27,600
can say. We just did not understand what we were doing. It's a great tragedy.

349
00:27:32,460 --> 00:27:37,140
The Sherman tank was named after Civil War Union General William Tecumseh Sherman.

350
00:27:39,180 --> 00:27:42,580
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

351
00:27:42,581 --> 00:27:51,160
40 miles outside the city of Denver, Colorado, stands what appears to be a sculpture of modern art.

352
00:27:52,120 --> 00:27:58,480
With its tint-like white peaks glistening in the sun, DIA, Denver International Airport,

353
00:27:58,481 --> 00:28:04,560
was designed to represent the perfect combination of aesthetic form and practical function,

354
00:28:05,620 --> 00:28:08,360
a state-of-the-art airport for the entire world to emulate.

355
00:28:08,361 --> 00:28:15,320
DIA's designers wanted to impress not only with architectural innovation,

356
00:28:15,820 --> 00:28:19,400
but with an advancement in technology that had never been attempted.

357
00:28:20,980 --> 00:28:24,700
A computerized, totally automated baggage transfer system

358
00:28:24,701 --> 00:28:27,860
that would move luggage from curbside to plane,

359
00:28:29,020 --> 00:28:32,500
plane to plane, and plane back to baggage claim

360
00:28:32,501 --> 00:28:35,640
with unimaginable speed and efficiency.

361
00:28:35,641 --> 00:28:49,641
The hope was that they would have a way to almost instantaneously move the bags from one aircraft to another inexpensively, without people, and very reliably.

362
00:28:53,800 --> 00:28:59,740
On March 10, 1994, Air Force supervisors were ready to show the system to the world.

363
00:29:01,060 --> 00:29:05,060
This is the world's largest, fastest, most sophisticated baggage system.

364
00:29:05,640 --> 00:29:13,160
It utilizes over 3,100 cars running on 20 miles of track.

365
00:29:13,161 --> 00:29:18,460
It's powered by over 10,000 motors, 100 computers, and the whole thing is interconnected with

366
00:29:18,461 --> 00:29:19,920
14 million feet of wire.

367
00:29:20,700 --> 00:29:21,960
There was only one problem.

368
00:29:25,280 --> 00:29:27,340
The system wasn't ready to be seen.

369
00:29:28,160 --> 00:29:31,440
It was a public relations and operational disaster.

370
00:29:31,560 --> 00:29:33,220
There's just no other words for it.

371
00:29:33,221 --> 00:29:38,700
The images that appeared in the papers at the time were of baggage,

372
00:29:38,980 --> 00:29:42,920
bags being chewed up, being opened, thrown on the floor.

373
00:29:43,240 --> 00:29:44,420
It was a mess.

374
00:29:46,760 --> 00:29:48,600
The pictures weren't staged.

375
00:29:48,720 --> 00:29:50,560
That's what really happened when they opened it.

376
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:54,860
They weren't ready for prime time when they invited the press in.

377
00:29:56,460 --> 00:30:01,180
The news media had a field day with the first display of the baggage system of the future.

378
00:30:03,220 --> 00:30:08,900
D.I.A., as in baggage, dead in arrival, became a popular phrase.

379
00:30:10,260 --> 00:30:14,180
Other terms used were the luggage eater, the D.I.A. debacle,

380
00:30:14,820 --> 00:30:19,480
and the greatest thing to happen to the carry-on baggage industry since wheels.

381
00:30:25,120 --> 00:30:32,860
I share your frustration that this magnificent new facility is not yet open.

382
00:30:33,220 --> 00:30:37,120
Because the system had been designed with no backup,

383
00:30:37,800 --> 00:30:44,000
the opening of the airport of the future was delayed into the future by nearly a full year.

384
00:30:44,780 --> 00:30:50,940
The estimated loss of revenue was a million dollars a day, all because of an engineering disaster.

385
00:30:52,380 --> 00:30:53,080
Are we all ready?

386
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:53,860
We're all ready.

387
00:30:53,861 --> 00:30:54,561
All right.

388
00:30:54,562 --> 00:30:56,980
Three, two, one.

389
00:30:57,580 --> 00:30:58,300
There we go.

390
00:30:58,301 --> 00:31:02,700
When DIA finally did open,

391
00:31:08,960 --> 00:31:11,340
the automated system had been relegated

392
00:31:11,341 --> 00:31:13,500
to backing up a more traditional system.

393
00:31:15,380 --> 00:31:17,160
Mann takes bag off plane.

394
00:31:20,940 --> 00:31:23,020
Mann puts bag on conveyor belt.

395
00:31:23,860 --> 00:31:26,000
Mann takes bag off conveyor belt.

396
00:31:28,300 --> 00:31:34,120
How could the creators of such a masterpiece of artistic design have produced such an engineering disaster?

397
00:31:35,400 --> 00:31:39,500
In order to answer that question, it's important to see this.

398
00:31:43,900 --> 00:31:47,160
A masterpiece of engineering and computer technology.

399
00:31:49,200 --> 00:31:54,060
Whizzing luggage along an incredible labyrinth of belts and tracks underground at DIA

400
00:31:54,061 --> 00:31:58,660
is in effect how the system was supposed to work,

401
00:31:59,600 --> 00:32:02,420
which happens to be how it's finally working now,

402
00:32:03,100 --> 00:32:06,440
at least for United Airlines flights.

403
00:32:10,280 --> 00:32:12,840
It's taken 10 years to solve the problems

404
00:32:12,841 --> 00:32:15,840
airport authorities originally hoped could be resolved in two.

405
00:32:17,340 --> 00:32:18,460
So what happened?

406
00:32:19,180 --> 00:32:20,260
Why wasn't it ready?

407
00:32:21,120 --> 00:32:23,520
I guess in some ways the major problem

408
00:32:23,521 --> 00:32:25,000
with the system was hubris.

409
00:32:25,280 --> 00:32:27,020
That is, people thought they could do it

410
00:32:27,021 --> 00:32:29,340
and they didn't have to deal with the reality.

411
00:32:29,960 --> 00:32:32,360
But in terms of technical things that happened,

412
00:32:32,460 --> 00:32:35,040
it was a whole range of issues.

413
00:32:36,420 --> 00:32:38,980
The DIA automated system was designed

414
00:32:38,981 --> 00:32:41,400
so that every bag would be carried to its destination

415
00:32:41,401 --> 00:32:43,480
by an individual telecar.

416
00:32:46,620 --> 00:32:48,840
A bag begins its trip through the system

417
00:32:48,841 --> 00:32:51,600
by having a barcode on its ID tag

418
00:32:51,601 --> 00:32:53,140
read by a laser device.

419
00:32:53,520 --> 00:33:03,780
At the time the airport opened, barcode readers were the single line variety, incapable of obtaining a read with one quick swipe.

420
00:33:05,460 --> 00:33:09,060
Bags were sent to the wrong planes or to no man's land.

421
00:33:10,940 --> 00:33:18,980
Today, conveyor belts deliver luggage through cobwebs of advanced laser beams that read barcode tags without the bag even slowing down.

422
00:33:18,981 --> 00:33:31,540
The technology has grown so much that these now, I don't know if you see them, but it's sort of a spider web where it's on a holographic disc and the diodes that read

423
00:33:31,541 --> 00:33:38,880
the barcode, there's six of these and that gives it so much more opportunity to read the barcode.

424
00:33:38,881 --> 00:33:43,880
The bag is then automatically placed in a telecar.

425
00:33:45,880 --> 00:33:50,940
Destination information read from the barcode is transferred to the car, so it knows what path to follow.

426
00:33:52,920 --> 00:33:56,000
Like taxis waiting for passengers on incoming flights.

427
00:33:57,380 --> 00:34:01,180
Empty telecars are always ready to jump onto the system to handle heavy traffic.

428
00:34:01,181 --> 00:34:08,740
At the system startup, the ability to handle heavy loads of baggage in peak travel periods

429
00:34:08,741 --> 00:34:11,520
just wasn't possible with the automated system.

430
00:34:12,900 --> 00:34:14,380
Today, it's no problem.

431
00:34:16,060 --> 00:34:20,860
Extra-large telecars take care of oversized baggage, like golf clubs and skis.

432
00:34:24,280 --> 00:34:28,740
Computers in a central control facility track the location of every telecar

433
00:34:28,741 --> 00:34:32,780
as it makes its way through a 17-mile maze of intersecting track.

434
00:34:35,200 --> 00:34:37,920
Accurate tracking was a major flaw in the original system,

435
00:34:38,480 --> 00:34:42,620
mainly caused by computer software, ill-equipped for the task.

436
00:34:43,260 --> 00:34:47,120
I can tell where the bag is in the system.

437
00:34:47,540 --> 00:34:50,980
I can tell the passenger that that bag belongs to,

438
00:34:51,400 --> 00:34:53,320
where the bag was input into the system,

439
00:34:53,960 --> 00:34:57,500
the city that it's destined for, the make-up unit,

440
00:34:57,501 --> 00:34:58,900
time the flight leaves.

441
00:35:00,180 --> 00:35:02,440
On their routes to and from planes,

442
00:35:03,300 --> 00:35:07,280
all baggage cars need to take a dive below runways

443
00:35:07,281 --> 00:35:10,300
into what engineers now call the hellhole.

444
00:35:10,760 --> 00:35:13,040
When the system was first put into operation,

445
00:35:13,840 --> 00:35:15,400
gravity took control at this point,

446
00:35:15,900 --> 00:35:17,960
allowing many bags to tumble off the track

447
00:35:17,961 --> 00:35:20,760
into the jaws of luggage-chomping machinery.

448
00:35:20,761 --> 00:35:30,380
Today, telecar speed is controlled by in-track magnets, which slow the descent.

449
00:35:31,580 --> 00:35:36,920
But by the use of these reverse magnets, we were able to slow that car down,

450
00:35:37,220 --> 00:35:42,320
so none of these bags fall out of the car while it's traveling down the hellhole.

451
00:35:43,140 --> 00:35:47,100
Right now, we're probably cranking anywhere between 10 to 12 miles an hour.

452
00:35:47,101 --> 00:35:50,880
By time we finish down there, we're anywhere between 3 to 5.

453
00:35:52,720 --> 00:35:55,860
Should any bag for whatever reason fall out of its car,

454
00:35:56,420 --> 00:36:00,400
detection devices using RF, radio frequency technology,

455
00:36:01,060 --> 00:36:05,320
immediately halt the system and inform operators in the central control center

456
00:36:05,321 --> 00:36:09,760
so the escapee can be captured and set on the right path.

457
00:36:12,340 --> 00:36:16,000
This form of RF tech was not available when the airport opened

458
00:36:16,001 --> 00:36:19,960
and has brought with it a whole new level of dependable speed to the system.

459
00:36:20,620 --> 00:36:23,820
It's a workable piece of engineering and marvel.

460
00:36:24,220 --> 00:36:28,200
I've been amazed by it since the opening of the airport that I've been here.

461
00:36:28,740 --> 00:36:34,220
The system can go anywhere from probably 1,750 to about 2,300 feet per minute,

462
00:36:34,660 --> 00:36:37,760
which still beats almost every conveyor system out there right now.

463
00:36:38,280 --> 00:36:39,280
It's very quick.

464
00:36:39,281 --> 00:36:47,760
Finally, each bag ends its automated thrill ride in a holding, makeup area where low-tech

465
00:36:47,761 --> 00:36:49,600
takes over.

466
00:36:49,601 --> 00:36:55,600
Human beings place the bags on old-fashioned trams to be loaded onto planes.

467
00:36:55,601 --> 00:37:03,720
With all the wide bodies, we probably see about 1,500 bags a day, so that would probably

468
00:37:03,721 --> 00:37:09,900
the end of the journey for the bag from its location to the terminal to out here.

469
00:37:12,680 --> 00:37:17,800
Although the automated DIA system is now working as it should have been 10 years ago,

470
00:37:18,520 --> 00:37:25,100
a backup system is in place. People-driven trams have their own concrete highway

471
00:37:25,101 --> 00:37:30,500
underneath the flying automated system to carry bags if the system goes down,

472
00:37:30,501 --> 00:37:36,920
or when flight delays require hot transfers, fewer than 15 minutes between connections.

473
00:37:38,460 --> 00:37:44,840
Simply put, the DIA baggage system became an engineering disaster for one reason.

474
00:37:45,720 --> 00:37:48,440
Too much was expected too fast.

475
00:37:49,620 --> 00:37:53,040
Today, even though the automated system is alive and well at DIA,

476
00:37:53,980 --> 00:37:58,480
it is only being used by United Airlines, which uses Denver as a major hub.

477
00:37:59,340 --> 00:38:02,940
That means computerized baggage handling, though working successfully,

478
00:38:03,900 --> 00:38:06,880
is handling only half the load for which it was originally intended.

479
00:38:08,120 --> 00:38:15,580
Since DIA installed the system, no new airport in the world has attempted to duplicate such a massive undertaking.

480
00:38:20,720 --> 00:38:25,940
The biggest problem for DIA's automated baggage system continues to be wheeled suitcases,

481
00:38:25,941 --> 00:38:29,320
which tend to slip and slide on the high-speed conveyors.

482
00:38:32,520 --> 00:38:36,400
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

483
00:38:37,500 --> 00:38:42,340
In Fairfax County, Virginia, the 26-story Skyline Plaza

484
00:38:42,341 --> 00:38:45,560
has been a popular apartment complex for more than three decades.

485
00:38:46,780 --> 00:38:51,640
During its construction, however, a tragic engineering disaster occurred

486
00:38:51,641 --> 00:38:55,380
that provided warning signals to an industry that thrived on building

487
00:38:55,381 --> 00:38:58,080
the tallest buildings in the shortest time.

488
00:38:59,780 --> 00:39:02,920
It happened on March 2nd, 1973.

489
00:39:04,400 --> 00:39:08,180
Construction workers were pouring the concrete floor slab of the 24th story.

490
00:39:09,840 --> 00:39:13,340
Suddenly, a contractor's worst nightmare.

491
00:39:22,480 --> 00:39:25,240
One of four structures making up the tower.

492
00:39:25,380 --> 00:39:34,100
collapsed all the way down to ground level 14 workers died in the disaster

493
00:39:34,101 --> 00:39:39,960
which left every member of the construction community asking why and

494
00:39:39,961 --> 00:39:44,560
Fairfax County District Attorneys wondering if anyone should be held

495
00:39:44,561 --> 00:39:50,320
criminally responsible my initial reaction was what in the world would

496
00:39:50,321 --> 00:39:57,760
make a building that's already gotten up to the 24th floor all of a sudden come tumbling all the

497
00:39:57,761 --> 00:40:07,040
way to the ground and i had i confess i had absolutely no idea and no theory at all the job

498
00:40:07,041 --> 00:40:13,840
of determining the specific cause of the collapse fell to evie leindecker structural engineer with

499
00:40:13,841 --> 00:40:19,920
the national bureau of standards and technology leindecker went to work to determine if the

500
00:40:19,921 --> 00:40:23,300
the tragedy could have been a result of poor design.

501
00:40:23,301 --> 00:40:26,020
He saw no immediate shortcomings.

502
00:40:26,021 --> 00:40:29,060
The most important evidence came from workers at the site

503
00:40:29,061 --> 00:40:30,540
who survived the collapse.

504
00:40:31,640 --> 00:40:33,240
From their eyewitness reports,

505
00:40:33,241 --> 00:40:35,320
he was able to piece together a scenario.

506
00:40:37,700 --> 00:40:40,940
Leindecker concluded that the collapse began shortly

507
00:40:40,941 --> 00:40:42,840
after a portion of the 24th story

508
00:40:42,841 --> 00:40:44,840
concrete floor slab had been poured.

509
00:40:46,920 --> 00:40:49,420
At the same time, wooden forms used

510
00:40:49,421 --> 00:40:54,618
cast vertical concrete pillars were being removed two floors below.

511
00:40:54,619 --> 00:40:56,480
According to engineering

512
00:40:56,481 --> 00:41:08,140
design recommendations, those forms were removed too early.

513
00:41:08,141 --> 00:41:14,760
The engineers' drawings had a statement on them that indicated that under a floor being

514
00:41:14,761 --> 00:41:17,520
cast or where the concrete was being poured,

515
00:41:17,521 --> 00:41:20,000
that you had to have a complete farming system.

516
00:41:20,001 --> 00:41:23,200
That means the wood that's supporting the concrete

517
00:41:23,201 --> 00:41:26,720
had to be in place underneath the slab being cast.

518
00:41:26,721 --> 00:41:29,760
And it also had to be in place for one story below that.

519
00:41:33,160 --> 00:41:36,540
The wet concrete being poured on the 24th story

520
00:41:36,541 --> 00:41:40,280
didn't have enough support because wooden forms were removed

521
00:41:40,281 --> 00:41:43,840
on floor 22, where vertical concrete pillars

522
00:41:43,841 --> 00:41:47,200
were not completely cured.

523
00:41:47,201 --> 00:41:50,300
Within days of the collapse, the question became not what

524
00:41:50,301 --> 00:41:53,440
happened, but who was responsible for carrying out

525
00:41:53,441 --> 00:41:56,260
the instructions stated on the engineer's drawings.

526
00:42:01,580 --> 00:42:03,420
Fairfax County building inspectors

527
00:42:03,421 --> 00:42:05,860
were not required to inspect the curing of concrete

528
00:42:05,861 --> 00:42:07,860
before additional floors could be constructed.

529
00:42:07,861 --> 00:42:15,580
It was the job of the contractor to make certain that vertical columns were secure

530
00:42:15,581 --> 00:42:18,640
before concrete was poured for the new floors.

531
00:42:19,700 --> 00:42:23,700
I could visualize people going in and pulling farms, and I'd say,

532
00:42:23,780 --> 00:42:26,580
hey, we're great, we're great, we're making good progress, let's just keep on going.

533
00:42:28,060 --> 00:42:31,680
That's always been one of my own conjectures, but that's all that is.

534
00:42:31,681 --> 00:42:36,040
But I think it probably was the inappropriate level of communication

535
00:42:36,041 --> 00:42:38,760
between the people running the construction site

536
00:42:38,761 --> 00:42:42,080
and the actual workers that were executing the work,

537
00:42:42,260 --> 00:42:45,680
but maybe not having explicit instructions on what they should be doing.

538
00:42:46,700 --> 00:42:49,600
When it became obvious that the skyline collapse

539
00:42:49,601 --> 00:42:52,000
had been the result of poor engineering supervision,

540
00:42:53,400 --> 00:42:55,880
the Fairfax District Attorney's Office mobilized

541
00:42:55,881 --> 00:43:00,000
to determine if any individuals were criminally liable.

542
00:43:01,400 --> 00:43:04,600
We ultimately presented a case to the grand jury

543
00:43:04,601 --> 00:43:08,440
to charge the superintendent of construction on that day.

544
00:43:09,160 --> 00:43:11,400
We charged him with manslaughter

545
00:43:11,401 --> 00:43:17,940
for what we perceived to be a willful, wanton disregard of human life

546
00:43:17,941 --> 00:43:22,480
when he ordered the shores to be pulled from the 23rd floor.

547
00:43:24,240 --> 00:43:27,240
Eventually, he was tried for the manslaughter,

548
00:43:28,060 --> 00:43:30,080
and the jury was never able to resolve it.

549
00:43:30,580 --> 00:43:32,400
The result of the trial was a hung jury,

550
00:43:32,401 --> 00:43:35,200
so there was no conclusion criminally.

551
00:43:35,600 --> 00:43:39,540
The Skyline apartment complex was completely torn down and rebuilt.

552
00:43:40,160 --> 00:43:44,680
The collapse made an impact on building inspectors locally and across the country.

553
00:43:45,560 --> 00:43:54,280
In the months after this happened, it seemed to me every high-rise building in the county under construction

554
00:43:54,281 --> 00:43:58,520
had three floors of shoring in place at all times.

555
00:43:58,521 --> 00:44:00,480
I think this scared the industry.

556
00:44:00,481 --> 00:44:06,980
it resulted in much more concern about safety than maybe had existed before.

557
00:44:08,140 --> 00:44:11,940
Today, as a direct result of the Skyline incident,

558
00:44:12,860 --> 00:44:17,500
building codes and contractor safety guidelines are more defined and enforced.

559
00:44:18,800 --> 00:44:24,000
No city wants to experience anything like the Skyline Plaza engineering disaster.

